course: everyday
lesson: 104
title: Growing up, honesty and manners
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: As children grow, their bodies start to change, and this happens to everyone, at different times and speeds.
explain: A parent, the school nurse or a doctor is the right person to ask about these changes, and nobody should be teased for growing faster or slower.
explain: Honesty and good manners show respect: telling the truth keeps trust, and lost things go back to their owners.
explain: For example, a girl in Brisbane who finds a purse in the school playground takes it straight to the office, because its owner will be looking for it.
fact: All bodies change as they grow, and it is normal.
fact: A parent, the school nurse or a doctor can answer questions about a changing body.
fact: Everyone grows at their own speed, so bodies change at different times.
fact: Telling the truth, even when you might be in trouble, keeps people's trust.
fact: Lies make trust hard to rebuild once people find out.
fact: Money or something valuable found at school should go to a teacher or the office.
fact: The owner of a lost thing will be looking for it.
fact: Staring or pointing at someone who looks different or acts unusually is disrespectful.
fact: Everyone deserves respect, whatever they look like.
fact: Please and thank you matter at home with your family, not only outside.
exercise: You notice your body is starting to change. Who is good to ask about it? | A parent, the school nurse or a doctor | Nobody, because it is far too embarrassing to talk about | An older child on the bus | A random video online
exercise: Is it normal for your body to change as you grow? | Yes, all bodies change and it is normal | No, it only happens to a few unlucky people | Only if you are ill | Only if you eat too much
exercise: A friend is teased because he is much shorter than others. What is true? | Everyone grows at their own speed | He will always stay short | Short children are younger | He is not eating enough of his vegetables
exercise: Some girls in your class are growing faster than others. Why? | Bodies change at different times | They are eating different food | They must be older than the rest of the class | They are doing more sports
exercise: You broke a cup and your mum asks who did it. What do you do? | Tell the truth | Blame the cat | Say you do not know | Hide the pieces
exercise: Why is telling a lie to avoid trouble a bad idea? | Lies make trust hard to rebuild | Lies always get you more pocket money | Lies are fine if nobody finds out | Lies are only bad at school
exercise: You find a ten-dollar bill in the school corridor. What do you do? | Hand it to a teacher or the office | Keep it, as finders are keepers | Spend it at the school shop | Give it to the first person you see
exercise: You find a nice watch on the school field. Why give it to the office? | The owner will be looking for it | The office keeps all lost things forever | You might get in trouble for having it | The watch is probably broken
exercise: A man on the bus talks loudly to himself. What is respectful? | Do not stare or point | Point him out to your friend | Laugh so he notices | Stare at him until he stops
exercise: A person in the shop has a large birthmark on her face. What should you do? | Not stare or point, and treat her normally | Ask her loudly what happened to her face | Point so your mum can see | Hide behind your mum and peep out at her
exercise: Your dad passes you the salt at dinner. What do you say? | Thank you | Nothing, he is only your dad | Hurry up next time | You took too long
exercise: Why use please and thank you with your family? | Manners matter with the people you live with too | Families never notice manners | Manners are only for visitors | It is only really needed when you talk to your grandparents
test: Ben is the tallest boy in Year 5 and gets teased for it. What is the truth? | Children grow at different speeds and that is normal | He has done something wrong | He should try to stop growing | Only tall children change as they grow, so he is the odd one out
test: Lily feels worried about changes in her body. What would help most? | Talking to her mum, the school nurse or a doctor | Keeping it secret from everyone | Asking strangers on the internet to tell her what is happening | Waiting until it goes away
test: Sam broke the window with his ball and said his sister did it. His dad found out. What is the likely result? | His dad finds it harder to believe him next time | His dad trusts him even more now, because he spoke up quickly | His sister gets a reward | Nothing at all changes
test: Which is true about finding something valuable at school? | You should hand it in so it can go back to its owner | Finders keepers is the school rule | You should keep it for a week first | You should sell it to a friend for a fair price
test: A girl in a wheelchair is in the park and your little brother points. What should you tell him? | Pointing and staring are not kind; she deserves respect | Point more quietly next time | Point only if she cannot see | It is fine to point at things that are new, because that is how children learn
test: Which shows good manners at home? | Saying please when you ask your sister for the remote | Taking the remote without asking | Shouting at her until she gives you the remote | Only saying please to guests
test: Why should you not stare at someone who acts unusually in public? | It makes them feel uncomfortable and is disrespectful | Staring is only rude at night | They might stare back at you | Staring at them makes them act even stranger than before
test: Mia finds a phone on the playground. She is tempted to keep it. What is the honest thing? | Hand it to a teacher or the office | Keep it unless someone asks | Turn it off and hide it | Use it herself until the owner turns up to ask for it
test: found a tenner in the playground, finders keepers!! | Give it to a teacher or the office | Keep it, nobody will know | Spend it on sweets with friends, it's a lucky day | Leave it where you found it
test: i broke the tv remote n mum asked, said it was jake | Tell the truth, lies make trust hard to rebuild | Keep blaming Jake, it worked last time so it'll work now | Say it was already broken | Say the dog did it instead
test: that man's talking to himself on the bus, look lol | Do not stare or point, it is disrespectful | Point so your friend can see | Laugh quietly behind your hand so he does not notice | Copy him to make friends laugh
test: do i hafta say please to my own mum, its just mum | Yes, please and thank you matter at home too | No, only say it to strangers | No, family do not need manners | Only when you want something big, like a new bike
explain: A boy in Texas lied that he had done his homework for weeks; when the test came he could not do any of it and had to stay behind for extra classes.
explain: A girl in Manchester told her friends she had been to Disney when she had not; when they found out, they stopped trusting her for a long time.
fact: Once people catch you lying, it takes a long time for them to trust you again.
fact: Hiding that you do not understand schoolwork makes the gaps bigger and harder to fix later.
fact: Private family news, such as relatives getting a divorce, is not yours to pass on.
fact: Liking someone «like like» means having a crush, liking them as more than a friend; a friend who tells you this trusts you to keep it private.
fact: When parents are not getting on, it is never the child's fault, and a trusted adult can help.
test: said i did my homework but i didn't, teacher believes me | Tell the truth and catch up, the gap gets bigger | Keep lying, no one will ever know if u keep quiet | Copy someone else's | Say the dog ate it each time
test: made up a story to sound cool, now everyone found out | Say sorry and tell the truth, trust comes back slowly | Make up an even bigger story so they forget the first one | Blame someone else | Stop talking to them all
test: going thru changes growing up, too embarrassed to ask anything | Ask a trusted adult or school nurse, it's normal | Never ask anyone, just figure it all out on your own | Only ask online strangers | Hide it from everyone
test: heard Ruby told everyone she went to Disney n she never went, now nobody believes anything she says lol, gonna post it. should u | No, posting it is unkind; the lesson is that lies cost trust | Yes, so that everyone knows not to trust anything she says again | Yes, but only to people who were not fooled | Yes, because lying means she deserves it
test: some kid found a wallet at school n kept it n the police came to his house, the boys say he went to jail. what's probably exaggerated | Going to jail; the lesson is to hand found things to the office | That he found a wallet at school at all, because nobody ever drops one | That the owner was looking for the wallet | That keeping found money is wrong
test: Leo's suddenly way taller than everyone n Kai keeps saying it's cos he's on growth pills, everyone's saying it. what's likely true | It is just rumour; children grow at different speeds and it is normal | It must be pills, since nobody could grow that fast in such a short time | Leo must be older than the rest of the class | Being tall early means something is wrong
exam: Identify the person or persons to whom questions concerning the changes of a growing body are properly addressed. | A parent, the school nurse or a doctor | Older pupils encountered in the playground | Strangers upon internet forums and chats | No one, as such questions are forbidden
exam: Whereas a sum of money is found upon the premises of a school, determine the appropriate course of action. | Deliver it to a teacher or to the office | Retain it unless the owner comes forward | Divide it equally among those present | Leave it where it lies for the owner
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the concealment of a failure to understand schoolwork. | It enlarges the gaps, rendering them harder to remedy | It permits the gaps to close of their own accord | It is advisable so long as marks remain adequate | It is without consequence of any kind for most pupils
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the timing of bodily changes during growth? | They occur to every person, at differing times and speeds | They occur to every person at precisely the same age | They occur only to those who are especially tall | They occur only to those who eat a great deal of food each day
test: cousin goes "u wanna know a secret? Uncle Dan n Auntie Rose are getting a divorce" | Uncle Dan and Auntie Rose are going to divorce; that is their private business, so do not pass it on | It means they are going to buy a big new house together and move in there soon | It means they are moving to another country far away and taking the cousins too | Tell everyone at the party
test: Mia told me she likes Tom "like like" n now Leo asks me "is it true??" | Mia has a crush on Tom and trusted you; say it is not yours to tell and keep it private | Tell Leo it is true, because he asked you straight out and lying is wrong | Make up a different story | Tell Tom about it straight away so he knows and can ask Mia out
test: mum told me "it's grown-up stuff" when i asked why dad sleeps on the sofa | Your parents may be having trouble getting on; it is not your fault, and a trusted adult can help | It means the bed is broken and they are waiting for a new one to arrive | It means dad is ill | Ask everyone at school what it means, then tell your mum what they said
